## Before you commit to a direction

Do not write down the first palette and pairing that come to mind. That first answer is the
average of every page ever written about a business of this kind, and averaging is exactly
where sameness comes from.

Draft at least three genuinely distinct directions for this brief before you write anything.
Each one is a palette — the four hex values — a pairing of a display id and a body id from
the library above, and one line saying what that direction claims about the business.

Distinct means they disagree. Three near-monochromes differing only in their accent are one
direction written out three times. Change what carries the page between them: a dark,
high-contrast direction against a paper-and-ink one against a single saturated hue; a
pairing that contrasts by era against one that contrasts by width. If two of your three
could be swapped without anyone noticing, you have two, not three.

Then commit to exactly one, on the strength of what this brief actually says, and write the
brand document as that direction alone. The document has no field for a runner-up and the
crew downstream is not handed a choice you have already made — the other directions were
how you found this one, and they are not part of it.

## The bar

The finished page has to read as though this business paid a studio for it. The test is to
imagine swapping in a generic logo: if the page still works, the brand document was too
vague. Specificity is the whole of your contribution — a locked palette, a named type
pairing, a named motion, an explicit do-not list, and a plan that says what each section is
for. Everything you leave open comes back as the default.
